WN.lb(14,{"mt_WcfaSfVT33":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A fair count needs…","choices":["one touch and one number per object","two numbers for special objects","only counting the gaps"],"answer":0,"hint":"Pair each object with exactly one number word as you touch it.","explain":"One-to-one means one touch and one number name for every object."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"The last number you say…","choices":["tells how many objects there are","is always one more than the set","only names the colour"],"answer":0,"hint":"Cardinality: the final number name is the size of the set.","explain":"After a fair one-to-one count, the last number is the total."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Counting gaps between grapes is…","choices":["not one-to-one counting of the grapes","the best way to find how many","required before you start"],"answer":0,"hint":"Number names should attach to grapes, not empty spaces.","explain":"Gaps are not objects in the set. Count the grapes themselves."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Someone says 1, 2, 3, 4 for three toys by double-counting one. The true count is…","choices":["3","4","5"],"answer":0,"hint":"An inflated spoken total does not change how many toys sit there.","explain":"Three toys remain three even if a double-count made the spoken total 4."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"You count {n} beads one-to-one without skips. How many beads are there?","answer":"n","hint":"A fair count's last number equals the number of beads.","explain":"Fair one-to-one counting of {n} beads means there are {n}.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A set has {n} counters. You skip {k}. What last number do you say?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Each skipped counter never gets a number, so subtract those from the true size.","explain":"{n} counters minus {k} skips leaves a spoken last number of {=n-k}.","params":{"n":[5,9],"k":[1,2]},"where":"n>k"},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Why touch each object once?","choices":["so every object gets one number name","to make counting louder","so you can skip the last one"],"answer":0,"hint":"Touching once helps you pair objects with number words fairly.","explain":"A single touch per object supports one-to-one correspondence."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Saying number names with no objects nearby…","choices":["is not counting a set one-to-one","always finds how many toys you have","fixes a double-count automatically"],"answer":0,"hint":"One-to-one counting needs objects to pair with the number words.","explain":"Reciting number names alone is not one-to-one counting of a set."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"One-to-one counting means…","choices":["each object gets exactly one number name","saying numbers as fast as you can","counting the spaces between objects"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of pairing: one touch and one number word for each object.","explain":"One-to-one correspondence pairs each object with exactly one number name — no skips or doubles."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"If you touch the same grape twice…","choices":["your count becomes too big","the count stays fair","you must skip the next grape"],"answer":0,"hint":"Two number words for one grape inflate the total.","explain":"Double-counting adds an extra number, so the total is larger than the set."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"There are {n} apples. You count fairly one-to-one. What is the last number you say?","answer":"n","hint":"In a fair count, the last number name equals how many objects there are.","explain":"With {n} apples counted one-to-one, the last number said is {n}.","params":{"n":[3,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A tray has {n} grapes. Someone double-counts {k} of them (says two numbers for each of those). What total do they say?","answer":"n+k","hint":"Start from the true count, then add one extra number for each double-counted grape.","explain":"True count {n}, plus {k} extras from double-counts, makes {=n+k}.","params":{"n":[4,7],"k":[1,2]},"where":"n>k"},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Skipping a grape in the middle…","choices":["makes the said total too small","makes the said total too big","does not change anything"],"answer":0,"hint":"A missed object never gets a number name, so you stop too early.","explain":"A skip leaves an object without a number, so the last number is smaller than the set."}],"explain":{"kid":["When you count, give each thing exactly one number word.","Touch it once, say one number, then go to the next thing. Do not skip. Do not count the same thing twice.","If someone touches a grape twice and says two numbers, the count is too big.","The last number you say is how many things there are."],"adult":["EYFS / Year 1 number: one-to-one correspondence when counting a set. Stable order of number names plus one touch per object. Words: one-to-one, count, skip, double-count, last number, how many.","Common mistakes: double-counting the same object; skipping an object; counting spaces or gaps; treating the rhythm of saying numbers as enough without matching to objects.","In the lab your child taps each counter once, spots a double-count and a skip, rebuilds a fair count, links the last number to the total, then faces challenge traps for those slips."]},"familyTip":"Line up five grapes. Ask: 'Touch each grape once and say one number.' Watch for a double-touch or a skip. Swap roles and let them catch your deliberate double-count.","keyIdea":"One-to-one correspondence means each object gets exactly one number name: touch or point once, say one word, then move on. Do not skip objects or say two numbers for the same object. The last number you say is how many there are.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Fair one-to-one count or not? Drag each clue, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["One-to-one","Not one-to-one"],"items":[["touch each grape once while saying numbers",0],["say two numbers for the same grape",1],["skip a grape in the middle",1],["point once, say one word, move on",0],["count the gaps between grapes as objects",1],["last number said equals how many grapes",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagoto","caption":"Flagship lab: tap each object once while saying numbers, catch double-counts and skips, fix a messy count, and keep the last-number rule.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_dmNvjroCPT":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"After counting 'one, two, … nine', someone asks 'How many?' What is the best answer?","choices":["Nine","Count again from one","One"],"answer":0,"hint":"The last number you said tells you.","explain":"The last number word, nine, is the total."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"How many stars altogether? ★ ★ ★   ★ ★   ★ ★ ★ ★","answer":9,"hint":"Count every star across all the groups.","explain":"3 + 2 + 4 = 9 stars."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Ten coins are spread out on a table. Another ten coins are stacked in a tower. Which has more coins?","choices":["The spread-out coins","The tower","They have the same"],"answer":2,"hint":"Count each set in your head.","explain":"Both sets have ten coins; the arrangement doesn't change the number."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"You count leaves and the last number you say is {n}. How many leaves are there?","answer":"n","hint":"The last number tells you.","explain":"There are {n} leaves: the last number said is the total.","params":{"n":[5,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Stars are in two groups: {a} on the left and {b} on the right. You count every star, one by one. How many stars are there?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Keep counting from the left group straight into the right group.","explain":"{a} and {b} more makes {=a+b} stars.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[2,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A tower of {n} blocks gets knocked over and the blocks scatter across the floor. How many blocks are there now?","answer":"n","hint":"Did any blocks disappear?","explain":"Still {n}. Moving the blocks doesn't change how many there are.","params":{"n":[6,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Jay counts 7 cars starting from the red one. Kim counts them again starting from the blue one. What should Kim get?","choices":["7","More than 7","Fewer than 7"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the starting car change how many cars there are?","explain":"Kim also gets 7. The order you count in doesn't change the total."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"You count shells, and the last number you say is {n}. How many shells are there?","choices":["1","{n}","{=n+1}"],"answer":1,"hint":"Which number did you say last?","explain":"The last number you said was {n}, so there are {n} shells.","params":{"n":[5,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"8 counters in a line are pushed together into a pile. How many counters are there now?","choices":["Fewer than 8","8","More than 8"],"answer":1,"hint":"Did any counters get added or taken away?","explain":"Moving counters doesn't add or remove any, so there are still 8."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Count the triangles: ▲ ▲ ▲ ▲ ▲ ▲ ▲ ▲ ▲ ▲ ▲ ▲. How many?","answer":12,"hint":"Count in a steady rhythm, one triangle per word.","explain":"There are 12 triangles."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Lily counts some toys starting with the teddy and gets 6. Jo counts the same toys starting with the car. What should Jo get?","choices":["6","5","It depends where Jo starts"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the starting point change how many toys there are?","explain":"The number of toys is the same whatever order you count them in, so Jo also gets 6."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"{n} counters in a line are pushed together into a pile. How many counters are there now?","answer":"n","hint":"Did any counters get added or taken away?","explain":"Moving counters doesn't add or take any away, so there are still {n}.","params":{"n":[5,12]}}],"explain":{"kid":["When you finish counting, the last number you say tells you how many there are.","It does not matter which one you start with. It does not matter how they are laid out. A line of 6 and a messy pile of 6 are both 6."],"adult":["This is the cardinality principle: the final number word names the size of the whole set. A child who recounts from one when asked 'how many?' can recite numbers but hasn't yet linked the last word to the total.","Two related facts: the order you count in doesn't change the total, and spreading out or bunching up the objects doesn't change it either."]},"familyTip":"Ask 'so how many is that?' after counting. If your child starts again from one, count together and stress the last word.","keyIdea":"The last number you say when counting tells you how many there are.","visual":{"type":"tenframe","caption":"Add red and blue counters. How many altogether? The ten-frame shows how close you are to 10.","opts":{"a":3,"b":4}}},"mt_cr_Hs13qvw":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which one is in the right order?","choices":["one, two, three","one, three, two","two, one, three"],"answer":0,"hint":"Start at one. What comes next?","explain":"One, two, three is the right order. The order is the same every time."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Three, four, … what comes next?","choices":["five","six","three"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say it from the start: one, two, three, four, …","explain":"Five comes after four. One, two, three, four, five."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Bear says: one, two, two, three. What went wrong?","choices":["He said two twice","Nothing, it is right","He missed four"],"answer":0,"hint":"Listen for a number that comes more than once.","explain":"Bear said two twice. Each number comes once: one, two, three."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Count on: {n}, {=n+1}, {=n+2}, … what comes next?","choices":["{=n+3}","{=n+4}","{=n+1}"],"answer":0,"hint":"Keep going one at a time. No skipping.","explain":"After {=n+2} comes {=n+3}. Every number gets its turn.","params":{"n":[1,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Six, seven, … what comes next?","choices":["eight","nine","six"],"answer":0,"hint":"After five the names are six, seven, eight, nine, ten.","explain":"Eight comes after seven. Six, seven, eight, nine, ten."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"The frog hops on stone {n}. Which stone is next?","choices":["{=n+1}","{=n-1}","{=n+2}"],"answer":0,"hint":"The frog hops forward one stone. Say the next number.","explain":"Stone {n}, then stone {=n+1}. Each hop is the next number.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Count back: three, two, … what comes next?","choices":["one","three","four"],"answer":0,"hint":"Counting back goes down to one.","explain":"Three, two, one! Counting back goes down."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Mia counts: one, two, three, five. Which number did she miss?","choices":["four","six","two"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say it slowly: one, two, three, …","explain":"Mia missed four. It goes one, two, three, four, five."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"One, two, … what comes next?","choices":["three","four","one"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say it out loud: one, two, …","explain":"Three comes after two. One, two, three."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Count with me: {n}, {=n+1}, … what comes next?","choices":["{=n+2}","{=n+3}","{n}"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say the next number. Do not jump over one.","explain":"After {n} and {=n+1} comes {=n+2}. Each number comes once, in order.","params":{"n":[1,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Bear says: one, two, four. What did he miss?","choices":["three","five","one"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count it slowly: one, two, …","explain":"Bear missed three. It goes one, two, three, four."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"We climb the stairs. We are on step {n}. What is the next step?","choices":["{=n+1}","{=n+2}","{=n-1}"],"answer":0,"hint":"One more step up. Say the next number.","explain":"Step {n}, then step {=n+1}. We go up one number at a time.","params":{"n":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Count back for the rocket: five, four, … what comes next?","choices":["three","six","five"],"answer":0,"hint":"Going back, the numbers go down.","explain":"Five, four, three, two, one. Three comes next when we count back."}],"explain":{"kid":["Let us count. One, two, three, four, five! The numbers go in the same order every time. Two comes after one. Three comes after two.","Do not skip one. One, two, four is a slip. It goes one, two, three, four.","Say each one just once. Then keep going: six, seven, eight, nine, ten!","We can count back too. Five, four, three, two, one. Blast off!"],"adult":["This topic, for ages 2 to 3, is about reciting the counting words in the right order: first one to five, then on to ten. It is the counting string that later counting, adding and number sense are built on. Saying the words in order is not yet the same as counting objects; matching one word to each thing comes next.","Children learn the list like a song. The first few words are fixed early, then a child may have a stable but unusual part, such as one, two, three, five, six, which they say the same way each time. That is normal. Keep modelling the full list in songs and on the stairs, and they will correct it with practice.","Common mistakes: skipping a number (one, two, four), saying a number twice (two, two, three), getting lost after five (six, eight), and rushing so the words blur together. Be careful not to drill. Slow down, count together, and pause so your child can fill in the next word.","In the lab your child climbs five stairs saying each number, drags the missing number into a counting train, spots where Silly Bear skips or repeats a number, hops a frog to ten, and counts back from five to launch a rocket."]},"familyTip":"Count the stairs every time you go up or down: one, two, three, four, five. Sing Five Little Ducks and Once I Caught a Fish Alive. Sometimes stop before a number, like one, two, …, and let your child say three.","keyIdea":"Counting words always come in the same order: one, two, three, four, five, then six, seven, eight, nine, ten. Say every number once, with no skips and no repeats. Songs, rhymes and stairs help the order stick. You can count back too: five, four, three, two, one.","visual":{"type":"counters","caption":"Say the numbers as you tap: one, two, three, four, five.","opts":{"mode":"count","n":5}},"lab":{"type":"flagsay","caption":"Flagship lab: climb and count to five, fill the gap on a number train, catch Silly Bear's slips, hop to ten and count back for a rocket.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_wH7tKnqwh7":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Dots flash for a moment: one at the top, two at the bottom. How many?","choices":["3","2","5"],"answer":0,"hint":"One and two make a little triangle. Picture it.","explain":"3. One dot on top and two below make a triangle of three."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"The plate is empty. How many dots are on it?","choices":["None","1","2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look closely. Is there anything on the plate at all?","explain":"None, also called zero. An empty plate has no dots."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"{n} children watch 2 buttons roll far apart. How many buttons now?","choices":["2","3","1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did a button come or go, or did they only roll?","explain":"Still 2 buttons. Rolling apart does not add or take away a button, however many children watch.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"1 big elephant or 3 tiny ants. Which group has more?","choices":["The 3 ants","The elephant","The same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each animal is one, big or tiny. Look at how many.","explain":"3 ants is more than 1 elephant. A big thing is still just one."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Sam is {n}. He sees 3 birds and says three fast, without counting. What did he do?","choices":["He saw it at a glance","He guessed a big number","He shut his eyes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fast, without counting one by one, is called what?","explain":"He saw 3 at a glance. Seeing a small group fast is called subitising. Sam's age, {n}, does not matter here.","params":{"n":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which dice face shows 3?","choices":["Three dots on a slant","One dot in the middle","Two dots in the corners"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the face with the most dots: one, two, three.","explain":"Three dots on a slant is 3. One dot is 1, and two corner dots are 2."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Show 2 with your fingers. How many go up?","choices":["2 fingers","1 finger","All of them"],"answer":0,"hint":"Put up one finger for each thing, until you have two.","explain":"2 fingers up show 2. All your fingers would be 5."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"3 socks in a row, or 3 socks in a pile. Which has more?","choices":["They are the same","The row","The pile"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count each group once to check. Is one group bigger?","explain":"They are the same. Both are 3 socks. A row or a pile does not change how many."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"You see 3 dots in a line. Then they make a triangle. How many now?","choices":["3","4","2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did any dot come or go, or did they just move?","explain":"Still 3. The dots only moved into a new shape, so the number stays the same."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Mia has {n} cars. She sees 2 big trucks and 3 tiny cars on a mat. Which group has more?","choices":["The 3 tiny cars","The 2 big trucks","They are the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at how many, not how big each one is.","explain":"3 tiny cars is more than 2 big trucks. Size does not change how many. Mia's own {n} cars are a different group.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A dice shows 2 dots. Which group matches it?","choices":["2 birds","3 birds","1 bird"],"answer":0,"hint":"The dice has 2 dots. Find the group with the same number.","explain":"2 birds match 2 dots. They look different, but both are two."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Leo is {n}. He sees 1 apple. How many fingers show the apple?","choices":["1 finger","All 5 fingers","2 fingers"],"answer":0,"hint":"Show the same number of fingers as there are apples.","explain":"1 apple, so 1 finger. Leo's age, {n}, is not about the apples.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Look fast! One dot. Two dots. Three dots. You can see how many. You do not need to count.","Three dots can sit in a line. They can make a hat shape. Each time it is three.","Push the dots far apart. Still three! Push them close. Still three!","A big ball is one. A tiny bead is one. Two big balls are less than three tiny beads."],"adult":["Seeing a small amount at a glance, without counting, is called subitising. Perceptual subitising works for up to about three or four items, and it is one of the first parts of number sense. The EYFS Early Learning Goal for Number asks children to subitise (recognise quantities without counting) up to 5; at three and four, aim for 1, 2 and 3.","Subitising links a quantity to its number name, which builds cardinality: the idea that a number tells how many are in a whole group. Dice patterns, fingers and small groups of toys all help. Change the arrangement often (in a line, a triangle, a slant) so your child learns the amount and not just one picture.","Common mistakes to watch for: thinking that spreading a group out makes more (the number stays the same; this is called conservation of number), judging by size so that two big things seem more than three small ones, and guessing a number at random instead of looking. Counting one by one is fine as a check, but praise the quick look.","In the lab your child sees dots flash on a plate and says how many, drags three dots far apart to find it is still three, matches dice faces to birds on a fence, shows small groups of apples with fingers, and sorts big and tiny things by how many."]},"familyTip":"At snack time, put out 1, 2 or 3 raisins, cover them with your hand, then lift it for a second. Ask how many. Swap the pattern each time, and let your child do it to you too.","keyIdea":"You can see one, two or three things fast, without counting. A quick look is enough. Moving things apart does not change how many. Big or tiny, each thing is one.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"How many at a glance? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["1","2","3"],"items":[["● ● ●",2],["●",0],["● ●",1],["⚂ dice",2],["⚀ dice",0],["⚁ dice",1],["✌ two fingers",1],["☝ one finger",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagglance","caption":"Flagship lab: quick looks at dots, spread dots apart, match dice to birds, show groups with fingers, and sort big and tiny things by how many.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_YwDSMqVcK4":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"How do you count the toys so you do not miss one?","choices":["Touch or move each one as you say a number","Look at them all and guess","Say the numbers fast"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about how you know a toy is done.","explain":"Touching or moving each toy as you say its number means none is missed and none is counted twice."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"'Give me 2 spoons.' When do you stop?","choices":["When I say two","When the spoons run out","After I say three"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count as you give them. Listen for the number you were asked for.","explain":"Count one, two, and stop. Two is the number asked for."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"Ben counts {n} cars, one number for each car. What is the last number he says?","answer":"n","hint":"Count from one, one number per car, and stop at the last car.","explain":"One number per car, so the count stops at {n}. That last number tells how many cars: {n}.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which count of 4 bees is right?","choices":["1, 2, 3, 4","1, 2, 4, 5","1, 2, 2, 3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each number once, in order, with no skips.","explain":"1, 2, 3, 4 says each number once, in order. The others skip 3 or say 2 twice."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"There are {n} stars in a row. You push them close together. How many stars now?","answer":"n","hint":"Did you add a star or take one away?","explain":"Pushing them close does not add or take away a star, so there are still {n}.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A dice shows 2 dots on top and 2 dots below. How many dots?","choices":["4","2","5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the top row, then the bottom row, and put them together.","explain":"2 on top and 2 below make 4. You can see it at a glance."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Mia counts 3 apples. Dad asks, 'How many apples?' What is the best answer?","choices":["Three","One, two, three","I need to count again"],"answer":0,"hint":"The last number you said is the answer.","explain":"She already counted. The last number, three, is how many."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Sam counts the ducks: one, two, three. How many ducks are there?","choices":["3","1, 2, 3","1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which number did Sam say last?","explain":"Sam stopped at three, so there are 3 ducks. The last number you say tells how many."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"There are {n} ducks. You count them, then spread them out. How many ducks now?","answer":"n","hint":"Did any duck go away, or did any new duck come?","explain":"No duck came or went, so there are still {n}. Spreading out does not change how many.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Kim counts one block two times. What happens?","choices":["She gets one too many","She gets the right number","She gets one too few"],"answer":0,"hint":"Counting a block twice adds an extra number.","explain":"Each thing gets only one number. Counting a block twice adds one extra, so the total is one too many."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"There are {n} fish. You count them from the other end. How many fish?","answer":"n","hint":"Each fish still gets one number. Does the start matter?","explain":"You can start at either end. Each fish still gets one number, so there are {n} fish.","params":{"n":[2,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Let us count ducks. Touch one duck and say one. Touch the next duck and say two. Go on: three, four.","Each duck gets one number. Do not skip a duck. Do not count a duck two times. Move each one to a new spot so you know it is done.","The last number you say is how many. You said four, so there are 4 ducks. You do not need to count them again.","Spread the ducks out, or push them close. There are still 4. When Mum asks for 3 spoons, count them out and stop when you say three."],"adult":["This is the start of number in the early years. In England, Development Matters for 3 and 4 year olds asks children to say one number for each item in order (1, 2, 3, 4, 5), to know that the last number reached tells you how many there are in total (the cardinal principle), and to recognise up to 3 objects without counting (subitising). US Common Core K.CC.B.4 sets the same goals for kindergarten.","Reliable counting rests on Gelman and Gallistel's counting principles: one-to-one correspondence (one number word per object), stable order (the words always come in the same order), cardinality (the last word is the size of the set), order irrelevance (start anywhere, the total is the same) and abstraction (anything can be counted). Touching or moving each object keeps track of what has been counted.","Common mistakes: counting an object twice or skipping one; saying the words faster than pointing; recounting or reciting the list again when asked 'how many?', which shows the cardinal principle is not secure yet; thinking a spread-out row has more; and, when asked to 'give me three', grabbing a handful or handing over every item instead of stopping at three. 'Give me' tasks are the clearest test of cardinality.","In the lab your child touches or drags ducks and frogs into a counted row, answers 'how many?' after hearing a count, moves 5 stars about and checks there are still 5, counts strawberries onto a plate and stops at the number, and names small dot patterns at a glance."]},"familyTip":"Play 'give me' at snack time: ask for 2 grapes, then 4, then 3. Watch whether your child counts as they hand them over and stops at the number. Then ask 'how many have you got?' and see if they answer with one number or count again.","keyIdea":"To count, touch or move each thing once and say one number for it: one, two, three, four, five. The last number you say tells how many there are. Moving the things about does not change how many. To give someone three, count as you hand them over and stop when you say three. Very small groups, like the dots on a dice, can be seen at a glance.","visual":{"type":"counters","caption":"Tap or drag each counter to count it. The last number you say is how many.","opts":{"mode":"count","n":4}},"lab":{"type":"flagcount","caption":"Flagship lab: count by moving each thing once, find that the last number says how many, move stars, count out strawberries and spot dot groups.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_oDf_HO1TKQ":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"3 big apples or 5 small grapes. Which group has more?","choices":["The 5 grapes","The 3 apples","They have the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the things. Do not look at how big they are.","explain":"5 is more than 3. The apples are bigger, but there are fewer of them."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"You match up two groups of toys. Every toy has a partner and none are left over. What do you know?","choices":["They have the same","The first group has more","You cannot tell"],"answer":0,"hint":"A toy left over would mean one group has more.","explain":"If every toy has a partner and nothing is left over, the two groups have the same number."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Mia has {n} blocks. Leo has {=n+2} blocks. Who has more?","choices":["Leo","Mia","They have the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count both groups. Which number comes later?","explain":"Leo has {=n+2} and Mia has {n}. {=n+2} comes later when you count, so Leo has more.","params":{"n":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Leo's 5 cars are squashed up. Mia's 5 cars are in a long row. What happens if Leo spreads his out?","choices":["He still has 5","He has more now","He has fewer now"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did he add or take away any cars?","explain":"Moving cars does not change how many there are. He still has 5, the same as Mia."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Mia has {a} cookies. Leo has 2. How many must Mia give away to have the same as Leo?","answer":"a-2","hint":"Match Mia's cookies with Leo's. Count the ones with no partner.","explain":"Mia has {a} and Leo has 2, so {=a-2} of Mia's cookies have no partner. She gives {=a-2} away.","params":{"a":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which word fits? Mia has 2 sweets and Leo has 4. Mia has ____ than Leo.","choices":["fewer","more","the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is 2 as many as 4? Fewer means not as many.","explain":"2 is not as many as 4, so Mia has fewer sweets than Leo."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"One plate has 1 grape. The other has 3. Which plate has more?","choices":["The plate with 3","The plate with 1","They have the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"You can see 1 and 3 at a glance without counting.","explain":"3 is more than 1, so the plate with 3 grapes has more."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"Leo has {b} ducks. Mia has 1 duck. How many more ducks does Leo have than Mia?","answer":"b-1","hint":"Pair Mia's duck with one of Leo's. Count Leo's ducks that are left over.","explain":"Leo has {b} and Mia has 1. After pairing, {=b-1} of Leo's ducks are left over, so Leo has {=b-1} more.","params":{"b":[2,6]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Mia has 2 grapes. Leo has 5 grapes. Who has more?","choices":["Leo","Mia","The same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Match each grape of Mia's with one of Leo's. Who has some left over?","explain":"Leo has 5 and Mia has 2. Leo has 3 left over when you match them, so Leo has more."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Mia has {a} cookies. Leo has {=a+2}. How many more cookies does Mia need to have the same as Leo?","answer":"2","hint":"Match them up in pairs. How many of Leo's cookies have no partner?","explain":"Leo has {=a+2} and Mia has {a}. Two of Leo's cookies have no partner, so Mia needs 2 more.","params":{"a":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"4 cars are spread out in a long row. 4 cars are squashed up close. Which row has more cars?","choices":["They have the same","The long row","The squashed row"],"answer":0,"hint":"Match each car in one row with a car in the other row.","explain":"Both rows have 4 cars. Spreading them out makes the row longer, but it does not add any cars."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Mia has {n} socks. Leo has {=n+1} socks. Who has fewer?","choices":["Mia","Leo","They have the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fewer means not as many. Which number comes first when you count?","explain":"Mia has {n} and Leo has {=n+1}. {n} comes before {=n+1} when you count, so Mia has fewer.","params":{"n":[1,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Look at two plates. One has 2 grapes. One has 4. The plate with 4 has more. The plate with 2 has fewer.","Not sure? Match them up. Put one of your cars next to one of mine. Do it again and again. If I have a car with no partner, I have more.","A long row is not always more. Spread out 4 cars and they make a long line. Squash up 5 cars and the line is short. But 5 is still more than 4.","Big things are not always more. 3 big balls and 5 tiny beads: count them. 5 is more than 3, so the beads win."],"adult":["This is early number: comparing quantities. Development Matters asks three and four year olds to compare quantities using words like more than and fewer than. By the end of Reception the Early Learning Goal is to compare quantities up to 10 and say when one is greater than, less than or the same as the other.","Children use three ways to compare. Subitising is seeing a small amount, up to about 3, without counting. One-to-one correspondence is pairing each item with one item in the other group, so the leftovers show which has more. Counting gives each group a number, and the number that comes later when you count is more. Equal means the same number, even if the things look different.","Common mistakes: judging by the length of a row, so a spread-out row seems to have more (Piaget found that many four year olds do this); judging by the size of the things rather than how many; and mixing up more and fewer. Fewer is the right word for things you can count, and less is for amounts like water. Be careful to use both, and to ask how they know.","In the lab your child says which plate has more at a glance, then drags each car to a partner and sees that the squashed-up row has one left over. They count big balls and tiny beads, add or take away cookies to make two plates the same, and pick the plate with fewer."]},"familyTip":"At snack time, give two people a few pieces each and ask: who has more? How do you know? Line them up in pairs to check. Then spread one pile out and ask again: has it changed?","keyIdea":"To compare two groups, look, match or count. With 1, 2 or 3 things you can often just see which group has more. To match, pair each thing in one group with a thing in the other: the group with things left over has more, and if nothing is left over they are the same. Counting works too: the bigger number is more. Spreading things out or squashing them up does not change how many there are, and big things are not more than small things unless there are more of them. Fewer means not as many.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Who has more? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🙋 The first one has more","🙋‍♂️ The second one has more","🟰 The same"],"items":[["🍓🍓🍓 or 🍓",0],["🚗 or 🚗🚗",1],["🍪🍪 or 🍪🍪",2],["🐟🐟🐟🐟 or 🐟🐟",0],["🍎 or 🍇🍇🍇",1],["⚽⚽⚽ or ⚽⚽⚽",2],["🧦🧦 or 🧦🧦🧦🧦",1],["🌼🌼🌼🌼🌼 or 🌼🌼🌼",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagmore","caption":"Flagship lab: look and say who has more, match cars one to one, count big and tiny things, make two plates the same and find fewer.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_fQNZfATC-y":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"Ben holds up {n} fingers. He touches each one and counts. What is the last number he says?","answer":"n","hint":"One number for each finger that is up.","explain":"He says one number for each of his {n} fingers, so the last number is {n}. That is how many.","params":{"n":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Ana counts her fingers from the thumb. Ben counts the same fingers from the little finger. What happens?","choices":["They get the same number","Ana gets more","Ben gets more"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do the fingers change when you start at the other end?","explain":"The fingers stay the same, so the count is the same. You can start counting anywhere."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"{a} fingers up on one hand and {b} on the other hand. How many fingers are up?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Count the fingers on both hands.","explain":"{a} and {b} more make {=a+b}. Count them all to check.","params":{"a":[1,3],"b":[1,2]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Ana is 3 years old. Which shows how old she is?","choices":["3 fingers up","2 fingers up","A whole hand up"],"answer":0,"hint":"One finger for each year.","explain":"3 years, so 3 fingers up. Count them: 1, 2, 3."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Five little ducks are on the pond. One swims away. How many fingers do you hold up now?","choices":["4","5","1"],"answer":0,"hint":"One duck went, so one finger goes down.","explain":"5 ducks, then one swims away, leaves 4 ducks. So 4 fingers stay up."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"You hold up all 10 fingers. You put {k} down. How many fingers are still up?","answer":"10-k","hint":"Put the fingers down one at a time and count what is left.","explain":"10 fingers with {k} down leaves {=10-k} still up.","params":{"k":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which one makes 5?","choices":["3 fingers and 2 fingers","3 fingers and 3 fingers","4 fingers and 2 fingers"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the fingers on both hands.","explain":"3 and 2 make 5. 3 and 3 make 6, and 4 and 2 make 6 too."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Ben has 3 fingers up. He counts 1, 2, 3, 4. What went wrong?","choices":["He counted one finger twice","Nothing, it is 4","He missed a finger"],"answer":0,"hint":"He said more numbers than there are fingers.","explain":"He has 3 fingers, so he should say 3 numbers. Saying 4 means one finger got counted twice."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"How many fingers are on one hand?","choices":["5","4","10"],"answer":0,"hint":"Hold up one hand and touch each finger as you count.","explain":"One hand has 5 fingers, counting the thumb. Two hands have 10."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"You hold up {n} fingers. You put 1 finger down. How many fingers are up now?","answer":"n-1","hint":"One finger down is one less.","explain":"{n} fingers, then one goes down, leaves {=n-1} fingers up.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Kit wants to show 6 on her fingers. What can she do?","choices":["5 on one hand and 1 on the other","6 on one hand","Put up 1 finger"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many fingers are on one hand? Is that enough for 6?","explain":"One hand only has 5 fingers. A full hand of 5 and 1 more on the other hand makes 6."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"One hand shows all 5 fingers. The other hand shows {k}. How many fingers are up?","answer":"5+k","hint":"Start at 5 and count on the other hand: 6, 7…","explain":"5 and {k} more make {=5+k}. Say 5, then count on for each finger on the other hand.","params":{"k":[1,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Hold up one finger. That is 1. Put up one more. That is 2. Each finger up is one more.","Touch each finger that is up. Say a number for each one: 1, 2, 3. Stop at the last one. The last number is how many.","One hand has 5 fingers. Want to show 7? Put up all 5 on one hand. Then 2 more on the other hand: 6, 7.","Put up 3 on this hand and 2 on that hand. That is 5! You can make 5 lots of ways."],"adult":["Showing numbers on fingers is part of early number in the EYFS. Development Matters asks three and four year olds to show 'finger numbers' up to 5 and to link numerals and amounts, and US Common Core K.CC.B.4 and K.CC.B.5 ask children to count to tell how many and to know that the last number said is the total. Finger patterns also build subitising: seeing a small amount without counting.","The key ideas are one-to-one correspondence (one number word for each finger), the cardinal principle (the last number said tells how many) and the part-whole structure of numbers. Showing 7 as a full hand and 2 more uses 5 as an anchor, which NCETM's Mastering Number programme calls '5 and a bit'. Showing 5 as 3 and 2 or 4 and 1 is early work on the composition of 5.","Common mistakes: putting up the right fingers but not checking, counting one finger twice or skipping one, thinking the first number said is the total, always holding up a whole hand, and trying to show 6 or more on one hand. A common adult slip is insisting on one 'correct' set of fingers; any fingers will do if the amount is right, and showing a number in more than one way is worth praising.","In the lab your child taps fingers up and down to show 3, 2 and 4, touches each finger that is up to count it, shows 7 and 9 with a full hand and some more, makes 5 three different ways on two hands, and puts a finger down each time one of five little ducks swims away."]},"familyTip":"Ask finger questions in everyday moments: \"How old are you? Show me.\" \"Show me 3 for the 3 grapes.\" Then count the fingers together, touching each one. Sing 'Five Little Ducks' and put one finger down each time a duck swims away.","keyIdea":"You can show a number on your fingers: one finger up for each one. To check, touch each finger that is up once and say a number for it; the last number you say tells how many. One hand has 5 fingers, so for 6 to 10 you show a full hand of 5 and some more on the other hand. The same number can be shown in different ways: 3 fingers and 2 fingers make 5, and so do 4 and 1.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"How many fingers? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["3","4","5"],"items":[["☝ 1 finger and ✌ 2 fingers",0],["✋ a whole hand",2],["✌ 2 fingers and ✌ 2 fingers",1],["3 fingers and 2 fingers",2],["4 fingers up on one hand",1],["3 fingers up on one hand",0],["4 fingers and 1 finger",2],["3 fingers and 1 finger",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagfing","caption":"Flagship lab: show numbers on fingers, touch each finger to count, use 5 and some more, make 5 three ways, sing five little ducks.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_BBMbJdees6":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"2 big balls and 5 small beads. Which numeral goes with the big balls?","choices":["2","5","4"],"answer":0,"hint":"Big things still count as one each. Count the balls.","explain":"There are two balls, so the numeral is 2. Being big does not make more."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Sam counts his toy cars: 1, 2, 3. Which card does he pick?","choices":["3","1","4"],"answer":0,"hint":"The last number you say tells you how many.","explain":"Sam said 3 last, so there are 3 cars and he picks the card 3."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"The door has the numeral {n} on it. Knock that many times. How many knocks is that?","answer":"n","hint":"Read the numeral on the door. It tells you how many knocks.","explain":"The numeral {n} means {n}, so you knock {n} times.","params":{"n":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Mia has the card 2. She puts out 4 spoons. What is wrong?","choices":["Too many, she needs 2","Nothing is wrong","Too few, she needs more"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the spoons. Is it the same as the card?","explain":"The card says 2 but there are 4 spoons, so there are too many. She takes 2 away."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which numeral is the number five?","choices":["5","2","3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Five has a flat top line and a round tummy at the bottom.","explain":"5 is the numeral for five. 2 and 5 can look alike, so check the flat line at the top."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"The cake has {n} candles. Which numeral goes on the birthday card?","answer":"n","hint":"Touch each candle and count. The last number is the numeral.","explain":"There are {n} candles, so the card shows the numeral {n}.","params":{"n":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"4 apples in a pile and 4 apples in a long line. Which group has more?","choices":["They have the same","The long line","The pile"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count each group. Does moving the apples change the number?","explain":"Both groups have 4 apples, so they are the same. A long line just takes more room."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"The group has 3 bears. The card says 4. What do you do?","choices":["Change the card to 3","Leave it, it is close","Change the card to 1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the bears first. The card must say that many.","explain":"There are 3 bears, so the card should be the numeral 3. Close is not a match."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which group of apples matches the numeral card 4?","choices":["🍎🍎🍎🍎","🍎🍎🍎","🍎🍎🍎🍎🍎"],"answer":0,"hint":"Touch each apple and count. Stop when you say the number on the card.","explain":"The numeral 4 means four. The group with four apples matches: 1, 2, 3, 4."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"The card shows the numeral {n}. How many blocks do you put next to it?","answer":"n","hint":"The numeral tells you how many. Count out one block at a time.","explain":"The numeral {n} means {n} blocks. Count them out one at a time and stop at {n}.","params":{"n":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"3 cars are spread far apart. Which numeral card goes with them?","choices":["3","5","1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Moving things apart does not add any. Count the cars.","explain":"There are 3 cars. Far apart or close together, it is still 3, so the card is 3."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"The plate has {k} apples. The card says 5. How many more apples do you need?","answer":"5-k","hint":"Count on from {k} until you get to 5.","explain":"From {k}, count on to 5. That is {=5-k} more apples, then the plate matches the card.","params":{"k":[1,4]}}],"explain":{"kid":["This is the numeral 3. It says three. Can you find three apples? Touch each one and count: one, two, three.","Now find the card for this group. Count the balls. You say 1, 2, 3, 4. The last number is 4. So the card is 4.","Spread the apples far apart. Count again. Still 4! Push them close. Still 4! Moving them does not change how many.","Big balls and tiny beads both count. Two big balls go with the 2 card. Look for numerals on doors, buses and cakes too."],"adult":["This is early number work on numerals and cardinality. Development Matters asks three and four year olds to link numerals and amounts, for example showing the right number of objects to match the numeral, up to 5. US Common Core K.CC.A.3 asks children to represent a number of objects with a written numeral, and K.CC.B.4 says the last number name said tells the number of objects counted.","Matching rests on three counting principles. One-to-one correspondence means one number word for each object. The stable order means the words always come in the same order. Cardinality means the last word said is the size of the group. Small groups of up to about 4 can also be seen at a glance, which is called subitising.","Common mistakes: thinking a spread-out group has more than a bunched one, which is a failure of conservation of number; picking a bigger numeral for big objects; answering with the first number counted instead of the last; and counting an item twice or skipping one. Children also mix up how numerals look, such as 2 and 5. Be careful not to count for your child. Ask them to touch each item.","In the lab your child puts apples on a plate to match a numeral card, drags numeral cards onto groups that are spread out, bunched up, big or small, finds the group with the wrong card, and matches numerals on a door, a bus and a birthday card."]},"familyTip":"Go on a numeral hunt: house doors, bus fronts, lift buttons and birthday cards. Each time, ask your child to read the numeral and then show you that many fingers, steps or claps.","keyIdea":"A numeral is the written sign for a number, like 3. It tells you how many. To match a numeral to a group, count the things one at a time. The last number you say is how many. Spread out or bunched up, big or small, each thing counts as one. So the numeral 3 goes with any group of three things.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which numeral goes with each group? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["2","3","4"],"items":[["● ●",0],["● ● ●",1],["● ● ● ●",2],["●      ●",0],["●  ●  ●",1],["●●●●",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagnumer","caption":"Flagship lab: match plates and cards to numerals, spread out or bunched, big or small, fix a wrong card, and read numerals around you.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_nvdpxAJTBG":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"Each child has 10 fingers. Count in 10s. How many fingers do {n} children have?","answer":"10*n","hint":"Say 10, 20, 30 … one number for each child.","explain":"Count in 10s, one number for each of the {n} children. You stop at {=10*n}, so there are {=10*n} fingers.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"A frog starts at 0 and hops 2 each time. Where is it after {n} hops?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Count in 2s, one number for each hop: 2, 4, 6 …","explain":"Each hop adds 2. Counting in 2s for {n} hops lands on {=2*n}.","params":{"n":[3,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Count in 5s: 20, 25, 30. What comes next?","choices":["35","31","40"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each number is 5 more than the one before. Jump 5 more from 30.","explain":"30 and 5 more is 35. 31 is a jump of 1 and 40 is a jump of 10."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Mia counts 5, 10, 15, 16, 17. What went wrong?","choices":["She went back to counting in 1s","Nothing went wrong","She should start at 1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the jump from 15 to 16.","explain":"From 15 she jumped 1, not 5. Counting in 5s goes 5, 10, 15, 20, 25."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which list counts in 2s?","choices":["2, 4, 6, 8","2, 3, 4, 5","2, 4, 5, 6"],"answer":0,"hint":"Check that each number is 2 more than the one before.","explain":"2, 4, 6, 8 jumps 2 every time. The other lists jump 1 somewhere."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Count back in 2s: 10, 8, 6. What comes next?","choices":["4","5","7"],"answer":0,"hint":"Going back, the numbers get smaller by 2.","explain":"6 take away 2 is 4. Counting back in 2s goes 10, 8, 6, 4, 2."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"There are 5 pairs of gloves. How many gloves?","choices":["10","5","7"],"answer":0,"hint":"5 is the number of pairs. Count in 2s, one number per pair.","explain":"2, 4, 6, 8, 10. Five pairs make 10 gloves."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"On a 100 square, where are 10, 20, 30 and all the tens?","choices":["In one column at the end of the rows","All in the top row","All over the square"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each row of a 100 square has 10 numbers. Where does each row end?","explain":"Each row ends on a ten, so 10, 20, 30 … 100 make one straight column."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Count in 2s: 2, 4, 6, 8. What comes next?","choices":["10","9","12"],"answer":0,"hint":"Jump 2 from 8. Which number do you skip over?","explain":"8 and 2 more is 10. You skip over 9 and land on 10."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"There are {n} pairs of socks. Count in 2s. How many socks?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Say one number for each pair: 2, 4, 6 … and stop at the last pair.","explain":"Each pair is 2 socks, so count in 2s for {n} pairs. The last number is {=2*n}, so there are {=2*n} socks.","params":{"n":[3,10]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"You have {n} coins. Each one is 5p. Count in 5s. How many pence is that?","answer":"5*n","hint":"Say 5, 10, 15 … one number for each coin.","explain":"Count in 5s, one number for each of the {n} coins. You stop at {=5*n}, so it is {=5*n}p.","params":{"n":[2,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which number is not in the count of 10s?","choices":["45","30","90"],"answer":0,"hint":"Every number in the 10s count ends in the same digit. Which one?","explain":"The 10s count is 10, 20, 30 … and every number ends in 0. 45 ends in 5, so it is in the 5s count but not the 10s."}],"explain":{"kid":["Socks come in pairs. A pair is 2 socks. Count the socks in 2s: 2, 4, 6, 8, 10. Say one number for each pair.","Think of a frog that hops 2 at a time. It hops over 1, 3 and 5. It lands on 2, 4, 6 and 8.","You can count in 5s too: 5, 10, 15, 20. A hand has 5 fingers. Count in 10s: 10, 20, 30. It is quick when there are lots!","Keep the same jump each time. After 15 comes 20, not 16. The last number you say is how many socks, not how many pairs."],"adult":["This is skip counting. In England, the Year 1 national curriculum asks children to count in multiples of twos, fives and tens, and Year 2 asks them to count in steps of 2, 3 and 5 from 0, and in tens from any number, forward and backward. In the US, Common Core K.CC.A.1 has kindergarten children count to 100 by tens, and 2.NBT.A.2 adds skip-counting by 5s, 10s and 100s.","Skip counting is counting equal groups. The child treats a pair as one unit of 2 (unitising), which is the bridge from counting ones to multiplication: 6 pairs is 6 lots of 2, which is 12. The counts have patterns worth pointing out. Multiples of 2 are the even numbers and end in 0, 2, 4, 6 or 8. Multiples of 5 end in 5 or 0. Multiples of 10 end in 0 and sit in one column on a 100 square.","Common mistakes: slipping back into ones part way, as in 5, 10, 15, 16, 17, often where the count crosses a ten; chanting 2, 4, 6 without moving a group for each number, so the words and the objects drift apart; answering with the number of groups (6 pairs) when asked how many things (12 socks); and losing the step when counting back, as in 50, 40, 39.","In the lab your child moves pairs of socks and counts 2, 4, 6 up to 12, hops a frog along a 0 to 20 track in 2s, pays 30p and 45p with 5p coins, colours the tens on a 100 square, and fills gaps in counts of 2s, 5s and 10s, one of them counting back."]},"familyTip":"Count in 2s when you sort the washing: hold up each pair of socks and say 2, 4, 6. At the door, count the shoes in 2s. Then ask two questions: how many pairs, and how many shoes? Count coins in 5s or 10s at the shop.","keyIdea":"Counting in 2s means jumping 2 each time: 2, 4, 6, 8, 10. Counting in 5s goes 5, 10, 15, 20, and counting in 10s goes 10, 20, 30. Keep the same jump every time. The last number you say tells how many things there are, not how many groups.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which jump does each count use? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Jumps of 2","Jumps of 5","Jumps of 10"],"items":[["2, 4, 6, 8",0],["5, 10, 15, 20",1],["10, 20, 30, 40",2],["12, 14, 16, 18",0],["25, 30, 35, 40",1],["60, 70, 80, 90",2],["20, 22, 24, 26",0],["45, 50, 55, 60",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagskip","caption":"Flagship lab: count pairs of socks in 2s, hop a frog to 20, pay with 5p coins, colour the tens on a 100 square and fill gaps in counts.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_sYpKWbq5ra":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A tower has {a} cubes. You put one more on top. How many cubes now?","answer":"a+1","hint":"Adding one cube gives the next counting number after {a}.","explain":"{a} and one more makes {=a+1}.","params":{"a":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"What number is one less than {a}?","answer":"a-1","hint":"Count back one step from {a}.","explain":"One less than {a} is {=a-1}, the number just before it.","params":{"a":[2,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Mia says one more than 4 is 3. Is she right?","choices":["No, one more than 4 is 5","Yes, it is 3","No, it is 4"],"answer":0,"hint":"One more makes the group bigger. Is 3 bigger than 4?","explain":"No. 3 is one less than 4. One more than 4 is 5, the number after it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which number comes just after 7?","choices":["8","6","9"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count 5, 6, 7, and say the next one.","explain":"8 comes just after 7, so 8 is one more than 7."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Jo has 3 stickers. Kit has one more than Jo. How many does Kit have?","choices":["4","3","5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Start at 3 and count on just one.","explain":"One more than 3 is 4, so Kit has 4 stickers."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A staircase has 1 block, then 2, then 3. How many blocks does the next step need?","choices":["4","3","6"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each step is one block taller than the step before.","explain":"Each step is one more. One more than 3 is 4, so the next step needs 4 blocks."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Ben has 5 grapes. He eats one. Which is right?","choices":["He has 4, one less than 5","He has 6, one more than 5","He still has 5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Eating one makes the group smaller. Count back one.","explain":"Eating one takes one away. One less than 5 is 4."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"8 ducks are on a pond. One more lands. How many now?","choices":["9","10","8"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say 8 in your head and count on one.","explain":"8 and one more makes 9. 10 would be two more."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"Sam has {a} blocks. He adds one more. How many blocks now?","answer":"a+1","hint":"Say {a}, then say the next number.","explain":"One more than {a} is {=a+1}. It is the next number when we count.","params":{"a":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"There are {a} birds on a wall. One flies away. How many are left?","answer":"a-1","hint":"One less is the number you say just before {a}.","explain":"One less than {a} is {=a-1}. It comes just before {a} when we count.","params":{"a":[3,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"What is one more than 9?","choices":["10","8","9"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count to 9. What number do you say next?","explain":"One more than 9 is 10. After 9 we say 10."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"There are 6 toys in a box. Mum puts in one more. What is the quick way to know how many?","choices":["Say 6, then say 7","Tip them out and count from 1","Say 6 again"],"answer":0,"hint":"You already know there are 6. Do you need to count those again?","explain":"Keep 6 in your head and count on one: 7. There are 7 toys."}],"explain":{"kid":["Here are 5 blocks. Put one more on top. Now there are 6. Put on one more. Now there are 7. Each time you add one, you get the next number.","So one more than 5 is 6. That is the number you say next when you count: 5, 6. One more than 9 is 10.","One less is the other way. Take one block off 7, and 6 are left. One less is the number you say just before.","If you know there are 6 in a box and one more goes in, you do not have to count them all again. Say 6 in your head, then say 7."],"adult":["This is the successor idea: each number in the counting sequence names a quantity exactly one larger than the number before it. The US Common Core puts it in Kindergarten (K.CC.4c, 'each successive number name refers to a quantity that is one larger'), and the English national curriculum asks Year 1 pupils, 'given a number, identify one more and one less'. Reception children meet it in the early learning goal for number.","It joins two skills your child may already have: saying the number names in order (the stable order) and knowing that the last number counted tells how many (cardinality). One more links them: moving one step along the count adds exactly one object. Counting on from a known number, instead of recounting from 1, grows out of this and leads to adding.","Common mistakes to watch for: saying the same number back (one more than 4 is 4); giving the number before (one more than 4 is 3); jumping two (4, then 6); and tipping out a group to count it all again from 1 when one more is added. Crossing a decade boundary is a classic slip too: one more than 9 is 10, and one more than 19 is 20, not 'ten-teen'.","In the lab your child drags cubes onto a tower and hears the next number each time, builds a staircase where each step is one cube taller, hops a frog one more along a 1 to 10 track, takes a cube away to find one less, and counts on from a number hidden in a cup."]},"familyTip":"On the stairs, say the number of each step as you climb: one, two, three. Stop and ask, 'What number is one more step?' Then come down one and ask, 'What is one less?' At snack time, put 5 grapes in a cup, show one more going in, and ask how many without tipping them out.","keyIdea":"Each counting number is one more than the number before it. Add one thing to a group of 5 and there are 6; add one more and there are 7. So one more than a number is the next number we say when we count, and one less is the number just before. You do not need to count a group again from 1: keep the number in your head and count on one.","visual":{"type":"counters","caption":"5 and one more. Push the groups together and count: there are 6.","opts":{"mode":"combine","a":5,"b":1}},"lab":{"type":"flagstair","caption":"Flagship lab: add one cube at a time, build a number staircase, hop one more on a track, take one away, and count on from a hidden number.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_M2v1A9OEuM":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"Count in hundreds: {=a*10+b}, {=a*10+b+100}, {=a*10+b+200}, … What comes next?","answer":"a*10+b+300","hint":"Only the hundreds digit changes. The tens and ones stay the same.","explain":"Add one more hundred: {=a*10+b+200} + 100 = {=a*10+b+300}. The last two digits never change.","params":{"a":[1,6],"b":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"Count in fives: {=a*100+85}, {=a*100+90}, {=a*100+95}, … What comes next?","answer":"a*100+100","hint":"The ones digit goes 5, 0, 5, 0. What happens when the tens are full?","explain":"{=a*100+95} + 5 = {=a*100+100}. The ones make a new ten, which fills the tens, so they make a new hundred.","params":{"a":[1,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"A frog hops in tens from {=a*100+50} to {=a*100+50+b*10}. How many hops is that?","answer":"b","hint":"Count on in tens and keep track of each hop on your fingers.","explain":"From {=a*100+50} to {=a*100+50+b*10} is {=b*10} more, which is {b} hops of ten.","params":{"a":[1,8],"b":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which number is 6 hundreds, 0 tens and 3 ones?","choices":["603","63","6003"],"answer":0,"hint":"Write a digit for each place: hundreds, tens, ones.","explain":"6 hundreds, 0 tens and 3 ones is 603. The 0 holds the tens place. 63 is only 6 tens and 3 ones."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"What comes after 599?","choices":["600","5910","590"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ten ones make a ten. Ten tens make a hundred.","explain":"599 + 1 = 600. The ones and the tens are both full, so you make a new hundred."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Count back in tens: 820, 810, 800, … What comes next?","choices":["790","700","799"],"answer":0,"hint":"Take away one ten. With no tens left, break a hundred into ten tens.","explain":"800 − 10 = 790. The hundreds digit drops to 7 and the tens digit becomes 9. 700 takes away a whole hundred."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"In 472, what is the 7 worth?","choices":["70","7","700"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which place is the 7 in: hundreds, tens or ones?","explain":"The 7 is in the tens place, so it is worth 7 tens, which is 70. That is place value."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Count in fives: 985, 990, 995, … What comes next?","choices":["1000","9100","996"],"answer":0,"hint":"Add 5 to 995. What happens when every place is full?","explain":"995 + 5 = 1000. Ten hundreds make one thousand. 996 only adds 1, and 9100 joins digits on the end."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"Count on in tens: {=a*100+70}, {=a*100+80}, {=a*100+90}, … What comes next?","answer":"a*100+100","hint":"Ten tens make a new hundred. What happens to the hundreds digit?","explain":"{=a*100+90} + 10 = {=a*100+100}. The tens were full, so they make a new hundred and the tens digit goes back to 0.","params":{"a":[1,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"What number is {a} hundreds, {b} tens and {c} ones?","answer":"a*100+b*10+c","hint":"Write the hundreds digit, then the tens digit, then the ones digit. A 0 holds an empty place.","explain":"{a} hundreds is {=a*100}, {b} tens is {=b*10}, and there are {c} ones, so the number is {=a*100+b*10+c}.","params":{"a":[1,9],"b":[0,9],"c":[0,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Count back in ones: {=a*100+2}, {=a*100+1}, {=a*100}, … What comes next?","answer":"a*100-1","hint":"There are no ones or tens left. Break a hundred into tens and ones, so the ones and tens go to 9.","explain":"{=a*100} − 1 = {=a*100-1}. The hundreds digit drops by 1 and the tens and ones become 9.","params":{"a":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Count in hundreds from 160. Which list is right?","choices":["160, 260, 360","160, 200, 300","160, 170, 180"],"answer":0,"hint":"Counting in hundreds changes only the hundreds digit.","explain":"Add 100 each time and the 6 tens stay put: 160, 260, 360. Jumping to 200 adds only 40."}],"explain":{"kid":["Big numbers can have three digits, like 345. Each digit has a place. The 3 is 3 hundreds. The 4 is 4 tens. The 5 is 5 ones. This is called place value.","Count on in ones: 197, 198, 199. What comes next? Ten ones make a new ten. Ten tens make a new hundred. So after 199 comes 200, not 1910.","You can skip-count, too. In tens: 650, 660, 670. Only the tens digit moves. After 690 comes 700. In hundreds: 150, 250, 350. Only the hundreds digit moves.","You can count back. 302, 301, 300, then 299. In fives, the ones digit goes 5, 0, 5, 0. Keep going: 990, 995, then 1000. That is one thousand."],"adult":["The US Grade 2 standard 2.NBT.A.2 asks children to 'count within 1000; skip-count by 5s, 10s, and 100s'. England's Year 2 programme asks for counting in tens from any number, forwards and backwards, and Year 3 asks pupils to find 10 or 100 more or less than a given number and to recognise the place value of each digit in a three-digit number (hundreds, tens, ones).","Counting is easy until a boundary. Going from 199 to 200 needs two exchanges at once: ten ones make a ten, and ten tens make a hundred. Counting back across a hundred, as in 300 to 299, needs the exchange the other way. Counting in hundreds from a number that is not a multiple of 100, such as 150, keeps the tens and ones digits the same.","Common mistakes: writing 1910 or saying 'one hundred and ninety-ten' after 199, writing 'three hundred and five' as 3005 or 35, jumping from 150 to 200 when counting in hundreds, and saying 290 instead of 299 when counting back from 300. Watch the zero: it holds an empty place, so 305 is not 35.","In the lab your child builds 305 and 460 from hundred flats, ten rods and single cubes, counts on from 196 to 203, hops in tens from 650 to 720 and in hundreds from 150 to 950, counts back from 303 to 297, and skip-counts in fives from 960 to 1000."]},"familyTip":"Count together on a walk or in the car, starting from an odd number like 186 or 640. Stop just before a hundred (197, 198, 199 …) or a ten-hop that crosses one (670, 680, 690 …) and let your child say what comes next. Then count back from 302 together.","keyIdea":"Every three-digit number is made of hundreds, tens and ones, and where a digit sits gives its value: in 305 the 3 is worth 300 and the 0 shows there are no tens. When counting fills a place, ten of it make one of the next place, so 199 + 1 = 200 and 690 + 10 = 700. Skip-counting in fives, tens or hundreds follows the same rule, and counting back undoes it: 300 − 1 = 299.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is the count going up in tens or in hundreds? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Up in tens","Up in hundreds"],"items":[["450, 460, 470",0],["690, 700, 710",0],["195, 205, 215",0],["980, 990, 1000",0],["150, 250, 350",1],["300, 400, 500",1],["605, 705, 805",1],["720, 820, 920",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaghund","caption":"Flagship lab: build 305 and 460, count on past 200, hop in tens past 700 and in hundreds from 150, count back past 300 and in fives to 1000.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_IzQvs7k_sE":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which of these numbers is in the 4s count?","choices":["24","26","30"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count 4, 8, 12, 16, 20 … and see which one you say.","explain":"4, 8, 12, 16, 20, 24: you say 24. 26 and 30 are in the 2s count but not the 4s."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"You count in 4s from 0. What number do you say just before {=4*a}?","answer":"4*a-4","hint":"Going back one jump means taking away 4.","explain":"{=4*a} − 4 = {=4*a-4}. One jump back in the 4s count is 4 less.","params":{"a":[3,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which number is in the 4s count but not in the 8s count?","choices":["20","16","32"],"answer":0,"hint":"8s numbers are every second 4s number: 8, 16, 24, 32.","explain":"20 is 5 fours, but 16 + 8 = 24, so the 8s count jumps over 20. 16 and 32 are in both counts."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"You count in 50s from 0. What number comes after {=50*a}?","answer":"50*a+50","hint":"Each jump is 50. Two jumps make 100.","explain":"{=50*a} + 50 = {=50*a+50}. The numbers in the 50s count end in 50 or 00.","params":{"a":[1,18]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"What is 100 more than 680?","choices":["780","690","700"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which digit changes when you add 100?","explain":"Add 1 to the hundreds digit: 680 + 100 = 780. The 8 tens and 0 ones stay the same."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What comes next? 56, 64, 72, …","choices":["80","76","82"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the jump between the numbers first.","explain":"The jump is 8, so 72 + 8 = 80. 76 would be a jump of 4."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"How many 50s make 400?","choices":["8","4","40"],"answer":0,"hint":"Two 50s make 100. How many hundreds are in 400?","explain":"There are 4 hundreds in 400 and two 50s in each, so 8 fifties make 400."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Count back in 100s: 920, 820, 720, …","choices":["620","710","700"],"answer":0,"hint":"Counting back in 100s makes the hundreds digit 1 less each time.","explain":"720 − 100 = 620. The tens and ones (20) stay the same."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"You count in 4s from 0. What number comes after {=4*a}?","answer":"4*a+4","hint":"Each jump in the 4s count is 4.","explain":"{=4*a} + 4 = {=4*a+4}. Every jump in the 4s count adds 4.","params":{"a":[2,11]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"You count in 8s from 0. What number comes after {=8*a}?","answer":"8*a+8","hint":"Each jump is 8. You can also do two jumps of 4.","explain":"{=8*a} + 8 = {=8*a+8}. That is the same as two more jumps of 4.","params":{"a":[2,11]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"What is missing? 250, 300, __, 400","choices":["350","310","500"],"answer":0,"hint":"What is the jump from 250 to 300?","explain":"The jump is 50, so 300 + 50 = 350. Two 50s make 100, so 350 + 50 = 400."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"What is 100 more than {=100*a+10*b+5}?","answer":"100*a+10*b+105","hint":"Add 1 to the hundreds digit. Keep the tens and ones.","explain":"{=100*a+10*b+5} + 100 = {=100*a+10*b+105}. Only the hundreds digit went up by 1.","params":{"a":[1,8],"b":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"How many jumps of 8 does it take to get from 0 to 40?","choices":["5","10","8"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say the 8s count and count the jumps: 8, 16, 24 …","explain":"8, 16, 24, 32, 40 is 5 jumps of 8. It would take 10 jumps of 4, twice as many."}],"explain":{"kid":["Skip counting means you jump the same amount each time. In 4s you say 4, 8, 12, 16, 20. Each jump is 4.","Two jumps of 2 make one jump of 4. So the 4s count is every second number in the 2s count. You skip one, say one.","One jump of 8 is two jumps of 4. So 8, 16, 24 and 32 are all in the 4s count too. To get to 48 you need 12 fours, but only 6 eights.","In 50s you say 50, 100, 150, 200. Two 50s make 100. In 100s you can start from any number. From 130 you say 230, 330, 430. Only the first digit goes up."],"adult":["In the National Curriculum for England, Year 3 pupils count from 0 in multiples of 4, 8, 50 and 100. They also find 10 or 100 more or less than a given number. This builds on Year 2 counting in 2s, 5s and 10s. It feeds the 4 and 8 times tables, which Year 3 pupils learn by heart.","The links matter more than reciting. Multiples of 4 are the doubles of multiples of 2, and multiples of 8 are the doubles of multiples of 4. So each 8s number is in the 4s count, but not the other way round (12 is 3 fours but not a whole number of eights). Counting in 50s alternates between numbers ending in 50 and 00. Counting on in 100s keeps the tens and ones digits, so it shows place value.","Common mistakes: losing the jump size and adding 2 or 10 by habit (16, 18, 20 instead of 16, 20, 24). Children also count the numbers said rather than the jumps, so they say 48 is 13 fours. Many expect the 8s count to need more jumps than the 4s count. Many round when adding 100, so 100 more than 350 becomes 400 or 360.","In the lab your child hops a frog in 4s to 48, taps the 4s numbers on a grid where the even numbers are marked, and races a 4s frog and an 8s frog to 48. They hop in 50s to 500 and count on in 100s from 130 to 930."]},"familyTip":"Count legs together. Each cat or chair has 4 legs, so count 4, 8, 12 as you point to them. A spider has 8 legs: count 8, 16, 24. On a walk, count in 50s or 100s from a house number or a bus number, and say only the hundreds digit changing aloud (130, 230, 330).","keyIdea":"Skip counting adds the same jump every time. Counting in 4s goes 0, 4, 8, 12 … 48; two jumps of 2 make one jump of 4, so the 4s are every second number in the 2s count. Counting in 8s goes 0, 8, 16, 24 … 96; one jump of 8 is two jumps of 4, so every 8s number is also a 4s number and it takes half as many jumps. Counting in 50s goes 50, 100, 150, because two 50s make 100. Counting on in 100s from any number only changes the hundreds digit: 130, 230, 330.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which counts land on these numbers? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["In the 8s count (so in the 4s too)","In the 4s count, not the 8s","Not in the 4s count"],"items":[["16",0],["40",0],["72",0],["12",1],["28",1],["44",1],["18",2],["30",2],["50",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagfours","caption":"Flagship lab: hop a frog in 4s to 48, tap the 4s on a 1-to-40 grid, race a 4s frog and an 8s frog, hop in 50s to 500 and count on in 100s from 130.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_7rJM8eWUfw":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Count back in 6s: 60, 54, 48, …","choices":["42","44","38"],"answer":0,"hint":"Counting back in 6s takes away 6 each time.","explain":"48 − 6 = 42. Taking away 4 gives 44, and taking away 10 gives 38."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which ones digit can a multiple of 6 never have?","choices":["7","4","8"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about whether multiples of 6 are odd or even.","explain":"Every multiple of 6 is even, so it never ends in 7. The ones digits go 6, 2, 8, 4, 0."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"6 × 10 = 60. What is 6 × {n}?","answer":"6*n","hint":"Start at 60 and add or take away a whole 6 for each step from 10.","explain":"6 × {n} = {=6*n}. Each step away from 6 × 10 is one 6, not 1.","params":{"n":[8,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which number is in both the 3s count and the 6s count?","choices":["18","15","21"],"answer":0,"hint":"The 6s are every second number in the 3s count. They are the even ones.","explain":"18 = 6 × 3, so it is in both counts. 15 and 21 are odd, so they are only in the 3s."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Packs of juice hold 6 cartons each. A class needs {=6*n} cartons. How many packs is that?","answer":"n","hint":"Count in 6s until you reach {=6*n}, and count how many 6s you said.","explain":"{=6*n} is {n} lots of 6, so the class needs {n} packs.","params":{"n":[2,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Ben counts in 6s: 6, 12, 18, 24, 28. What went wrong?","choices":["He added 4, not 6","He added 6 too many","Nothing, it is right"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the jump from 24 to 28 and compare it with 6.","explain":"24 + 6 = 30. Ben added 4, slipping into the 4s count, so 28 is wrong."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"How many eggs are in 5 boxes of 6?","choices":["30","11","25"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count in 6s five times: 6, 12, …","explain":"6, 12, 18, 24, 30: 5 boxes of 6 is 30. Adding 5 + 6 gives 11, which counts only one box."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which list shows the ones digits of 6, 12, 18, 24, 30?","choices":["6, 2, 8, 4, 0","6, 2, 8, 4, 5","6, 1, 8, 2, 3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Write the last digit of each number in the list.","explain":"6, 12, 18, 24, 30 end in 6, 2, 8, 4, 0. After 30, the pattern starts again with 36."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What comes next? 24, 30, 36, …","choices":["42","40","46"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each number in the 6s count is 6 more than the one before.","explain":"36 + 6 = 42. A jump of 4 would give 40, and a jump of 10 would give 46."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"There are {n} egg boxes. Each box holds 6 eggs. How many eggs are there?","answer":"6*n","hint":"Count in 6s, once for each box, or work out {n} × 6.","explain":"{n} boxes of 6 is {n} × 6 = {=6*n} eggs. Adding {n} + 6 would not count every egg.","params":{"n":[2,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"3 × {n} = {=3*n}. Use it to find 6 × {n}.","answer":"6*n","hint":"A 6 is two 3s, so 6 × {n} is double 3 × {n}.","explain":"Double {=3*n}: {=3*n} + {=3*n} = {=6*n}, so 6 × {n} = {=6*n}.","params":{"n":[2,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which number is NOT in the 6s count?","choices":["38","36","42"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count in 6s past 30: 30, 36, then 6 more.","explain":"30, 36, 42: 38 is skipped. It is even, but it is not a multiple of 6."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"6 × 10 = 60. What is 6 × 9?","choices":["54","59","50"],"answer":0,"hint":"6 × 9 is one 6 less than 6 × 10.","explain":"Take away one whole 6, not 1: 60 − 6 = 54."}],"explain":{"kid":["An egg box holds 6 eggs. One box is 6. Two boxes is 12. Each new box adds 6 more. That is counting in 6s: 6, 12, 18, 24, 30.","Look at the last digit of each number. It goes 6, 2, 8, 4, 0, and then 6, 2, 8, 4, 0 again. It is always even. A number that ends in 5 or 3 is never in the 6s.","Two 3s make a 6. So if you know 3 × 7 = 21, double it: 6 × 7 = 42.","You know 10 boxes is 60. Take one box off and you have 9 boxes: 60 take away 6 is 54. Take off a whole 6, not just 1."],"adult":["In England, Year 4 pupils are expected to count in multiples of 6, 7, 9, 25 and 1000 and to recall multiplication facts up to 12 × 12. Counting in 6s is the bridge into the 6 times table. Useful words: multiple, count in multiples of 6, times table, product, sequence, ones digit, double, even.","Two facts make the 6s easier to hold. First, 6 = 2 × 3, so every multiple of 6 is a multiple of 3 and of 2: the 6s are the even numbers in the 3s count, and 6 × n is double 3 × n. Second, 6 × 10 = 60, so nearby facts come from adding or taking away one 6: 6 × 9 = 54 and 6 × 11 = 66. The ones digits cycle 6, 2, 8, 4, 0 every five steps because 6 × 5 = 30 ends in 0.","Common mistakes: adding the numbers instead of multiplying (4 boxes of 6 read as 4 + 6 = 10); slipping into a jump of 4 or 10 partway through a count, such as 36, 40; taking away 1 instead of one 6 when bridging from 60 (writing 59 for 6 × 9); and thinking every number in the 3s count is also in the 6s count, so 15 or 21 creep in.","In the lab your child adds egg boxes to count in 6s to 30, fills gaps in a 6s sequence, taps the ones digit of each multiple to draw the 6-2-8-4-0 pattern on a digit wheel, joins pairs of 3s into 6s, and takes one box off 10 boxes to find 6 × 9."]},"familyTip":"At the shops, count egg boxes or packs of 6 drinks in 6s out loud. Then ask a quick bridge question: 'Ten packs is 60 cans. How many are in nine packs?' Let your child say 'one 6 less' before working it out.","keyIdea":"Counting in 6s adds 6 each time: 6, 12, 18, 24, 30, 36, 42, 48, 54, 60, 66, 72. These are the multiples of 6, the answers in the 6 times table. Every multiple of 6 is even, and the ones digits repeat 6, 2, 8, 4, 0. A 6 is two 3s, so the 6s are every second number in the 3s count and 6 × n is double 3 × n. From 6 × 10 = 60 you can find 6 × 9 = 54 by taking away one 6.","visual":{"type":"array","caption":"Rows of 6: 4 rows of 6 is 6 + 6 + 6 + 6 = 24. Change the rows to count on in 6s.","opts":{"rows":4,"cols":6}},"lab":{"type":"flagsixes","caption":"Flagship lab: add egg boxes of 6, fill gaps in the 6s count, trace the ones digits on a wheel, join 3s into 6s and take a box off 6 × 10.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_pAcaehday5":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Representing 2 means…","choices":["placing exactly 2 objects","placing 20 to be safe","placing none and saying two"],"answer":0,"hint":"Match the object count to the numeral.","explain":"Exactly 2 objects represent the numeral 2. Extras or empty trays do not."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which tray represents 4?","choices":["four counters","one counter shaped like 4","six counters for luck"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the objects on the tray.","explain":"Four counters represent 4. A 4-shaped object or extras do not."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Same number means…","choices":["same count of objects","same colour toys","same size toys"],"answer":0,"hint":"Focus on how many, not how they look.","explain":"Equal counts represent the same number. Colour and size do not change cardinality."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A digit is…","choices":["a symbol for a count","a pile of loops to count","always larger when curlier"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think symbol versus quantity.","explain":"Digits stand for counts. Do not treat digit curves as objects."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Build numeral {n} with counters. How many counters?","answer":"n","hint":"Use one counter for each unit the numeral names.","explain":"Place {n} counters to represent {n}.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Left tray {n}, right tray {k} (right has more). How many more on the right?","answer":"k-n","hint":"Subtract the smaller count from the larger count.","explain":"{k} − {n} = {=k-n} more on the right tray.","params":{"n":[2,4],"k":[5,8]},"where":"k>n"},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Tray has 6; numeral is 4. You should…","choices":["take 2 away","add 4 more","ignore the numeral"],"answer":0,"hint":"Change the tray until the count matches.","explain":"6 − 2 = 4. Taking two away makes the tray represent 4."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Why match object counts to numerals?","choices":["so the symbol and quantity agree","so the tray looks full","so toys stay pretty"],"answer":0,"hint":"Representation links numeral and quantity.","explain":"Cardinality links the numeral to how many objects you show. Fullness and looks are not the goal."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"To represent 5, place…","choices":["exactly 5 objects","1 object shaped like 5","extra objects for luck"],"answer":0,"hint":"The count of objects must match the numeral.","explain":"Representation means the object count equals the numeral. Shape tricks and extras change the number."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"3 apples and 3 cars…","choices":["show the same number","cannot match because they look different","show 6 because you add them"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare the counts, not the kinds of objects.","explain":"Both sides have 3, so they represent the same number. Kind of object does not change the count."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A tray must show {n}. It has {k} counters. How many should you take away?","answer":"k-n","hint":"Subtract the target numeral from the counters on the tray.","explain":"You have {k} and need {n}, so take away {=k-n}.","params":{"n":[2,4],"k":[5,8]},"where":"k>n"},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Numeral {n}. Tray has {k}. How many counters must you add?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Find how many more to reach the numeral.","explain":"Need {n}; have {k}; add {=n-k}.","params":{"n":[4,7],"k":[1,3]},"where":"n>k"},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"The loops in a written 8…","choices":["are not objects to count for eight","mean you only need 2 counters","replace the need for objects"],"answer":0,"hint":"A digit is a symbol for a count.","explain":"Digits are symbols. Represent 8 with eight objects, not by counting loops in the digit."}],"explain":{"kid":["A number like 4 means four things. You can show it with four counters, four spoons or four fingers.","Two groups show the same number when they have the same count — even if one group is apples and the other is cars.","The written digit is a symbol. Do not count the loops in an 8. Count the objects you place.","If a tray has too many, take some away. If it has too few, add some. Stop when the count matches the numeral."],"adult":["EYFS / Year 1 number: cardinality and representation with concrete manipulatives. Link numeral → quantity. Words: represent, count, numeral, digit, same number, too many, too few.","Common mistakes: counting loops in a digit; adding extras 'for luck'; matching by object kind or colour instead of count; thinking a single object shaped like a 4 represents 4.","In the lab your child builds a shown numeral, picks the tray with the right count, judges same vs different across object kinds, fixes a tray, matches digit to object count, then faces challenge traps for those slips."]},"familyTip":"Show a numeral card (e.g. 4). Ask: 'Can you put out that many spoons?' Then mix apple counters and car counters: 'Do 3 apples and 3 cars show the same number?'","keyIdea":"A numeral stands for a count (cardinality). Represent it by placing exactly that many objects. Matching collections share the same count, even if the objects look different. The digit shape is a symbol — do not count its loops.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does it represent 4? Drag each clue, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Represents 4","Does not represent 4"],"items":[["four counters",0],["one counter shaped like a 4",1],["2 apples and 2 cars",0],["six counters for luck",1],["four fingers held up",0],["the curly loops in a written 8",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagrepnum","caption":"Flagship lab: build a numeral with counters, match equal counts, fix trays, and catch digit-shape slips.","opts":{"step":0}}}});
